# Network Attacks and Defence in Depth

> Once a machine is on a network, strangers can touch it. This lesson
> maps the main attack families — floods, interception, spoofing — and
> the layered defences that answer them: firewalls, IDS, IPS, VPNs.

## 1. Know the Adversary

Defences make sense once you know who is attacking and why.

| Attacker | Motive | Typical behaviour |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Script kiddie | curiosity, bragging | reuses other people's tools |
| Cybercriminal | money | ransomware, fraud, data theft |
| Hacktivist | ideology | defacement, leaks, DDoS protests |
| Nation-state actor | espionage, disruption | patient, well-funded APT campaigns |
| Insider | grievance or greed | misuses legitimate access |

[info title="Why insiders are dangerous"]
Insiders already hold valid credentials and sit behind the firewall,
so perimeter defences see nothing unusual. This is one reason modern
architecture stops trusting location and starts verifying every
action — the zero-trust idea in Section 7.
[/info]

## 2. A Taxonomy of Network Attacks

Network attacks fall into two grand families: **passive** attacks
listen without changing anything, and **active** attacks alter,
flood, or inject.

@diagram tree id="network-attack-taxonomy" layout="tree" aria="Network attacks divided into passive and active families"
  "Network attacks" -> "Passive: eavesdropping and sniffing"
  "Network attacks" -> "Passive: reconnaissance and port scans"
  "Network attacks" -> "Active: DoS and DDoS floods"
  "Network attacks" -> "Active: man in the middle"
  "Network attacks" -> "Active: spoofing and poisoning"
  "Network attacks" -> "Active: injection and exploit attempts"
@/diagram

Passive attacks are hard to detect because nothing breaks — the
defence is encryption, which makes intercepted traffic useless.
Active attacks announce themselves eventually, so the defence is
detection plus filtering.

## 3. Denial of Service and DDoS

A **Denial of Service (DoS)** attack exhausts a resource — bandwidth,
connections, CPU — so genuine users get nothing. A **Distributed DoS
(DDoS)** does it from thousands of hijacked machines at once: a
**botnet** of infected laptops, routers, and cameras, each sending
mere trickle, together a flood.

| Vector | How it works | Layer |
| --- | --- | --- |
| UDP flood | junk UDP packets swamp bandwidth | network |
| SYN flood | half-open TCP connections fill the table | transport |
| DNS amplification | small queries trigger huge replies aimed at the victim | reflection |
| HTTP flood | expensive page or API requests exhaust the app | application |

@chart bar title="Illustrative share of DDoS attacks by vector" labels:
UDP floods
SYN floods
HTTP application floods
DNS amplification
Other vectors

data:
35
25
20
12
8
@/chart

Defences mirror the layers: scrubbing centres and CDNs absorb
volumetric floods, **SYN cookies** neutralise half-open connection
attacks, and rate limiting plus caching blunt application floods.
Amplification is discouraged by closing open resolvers and filtering
spoofed source addresses.

## 4. Man in the Middle and Spoofing

In a **man-in-the-middle (MitM)** attack the attacker silently sits
between two parties, reading or editing traffic. On a LAN,
**ARP poisoning** tricks machines into sending traffic through the
attacker; on a network, **DNS spoofing** hands out false addresses;
on public Wi-Fi, an **evil twin** access point invites victims to
connect to the attacker directly.

[example]
You join free airport Wi-Fi. The attacker's laptop relays every
packet. Sites without HTTPS leak logins in cleartext; even with
HTTPS, an attacker who tricks you into installing a rogue
"certificate" could decrypt everything — which is why browser
certificate warnings must never be clicked through.
[/example]

[warning]
A common misconception: a VPN or HTTPS makes you anonymous. They make
traffic unreadable and authenticated, not invisible — the sites you
visit, DNS queries outside the tunnel, and your IP address are still
observable.
[/warning]

The standing defence is TLS everywhere, certificate pinning where it
matters, and treating any certificate warning as an alarm rather than
a nuisance.

## 5. Firewalls: Gatekeepers With Rules

A **firewall** permits or blocks traffic between network zones
according to rules. The types form a short evolution:

| Type | Decides by | Knows about conversations? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Packet filtering | IP addresses, ports, protocol | no |
| Stateful | rules plus connection state table | yes |
| Application / NGFW | application behaviour, users, identities | deeply |
| Web application firewall (WAF) | HTTP requests and payloads | web layer only |

@diagram flowchart id="firewall-decision" layout="hierarchical" aria="Stateful firewall decision path for an arriving packet"
node arrive label="Packet arrives at the firewall" shape="pill"
node state label="Connection already in state table?" shape="diamond"
node rule label="Matches an allow rule?" shape="diamond"
node allow label="Forward the packet" shape="round-rect"
node deny label="Drop and log" shape="round-rect"

arrive -> state
state -> allow label="yes"
state -> rule label="no"
rule -> allow label="yes"
rule -> deny label="no"
@/diagram

The flowchart encodes the golden rule: **default deny**. Anything not
explicitly allowed is dropped and logged, and replies to connections
you started pass freely because the state table remembers them.

## 6. IDS and IPS: Detect and Prevent

Firewalls decide by address and port; **intrusion detection** looks
inside traffic for hostile behaviour.

- An **IDS** watches copies of traffic and raises alerts —
  detection only, no blocking.
- An **IPS** sits inline and stops the malicious flow in real time.
- **NIDS** monitors network segments; **HIDS** watches one host's
  logs, files, and processes.

| Approach | How it recognises attacks | Weakness |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Signature-based | matches known attack patterns | blind to brand-new attacks |
| Anomaly-based | learns normal, flags deviations | false positives on genuine change |

[tip]
Memory hook: IDS *informs*, IPS *intervenes*. Signature systems know
yesterday's attacks; anomaly systems notice anything unfamiliar —
including tomorrow's.
[/tip]

## 7. Zero Trust, Segmentation, and VPNs

The old model — hard shell, soft inside — fails when one phished
laptop gets inside. **Zero trust** removes implicit trust entirely:
every request is authenticated, authorised, and encrypted regardless
of where it comes from.

| Idea | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Never trust, always verify | no free pass for "internal" traffic |
| Least privilege | minimal access per identity, per session |
| Microsegmentation | workloads isolated into small zones |
| Continuous monitoring | trust is re-earned, not assumed |

A **VPN** builds an encrypted tunnel from your device to a gateway:
on public Wi-Fi it shields traffic from local snoops and gives remote
workers safe passage into company networks. In zero-trust designs,
per-application access brokers increasingly replace the
all-or-nothing network VPN.

## 8. Defence in Depth in Practice

No single control survives contact with a determined attacker; depth
means overlapping layers, each buying time and visibility.

| Layer | Controls |
| --- | --- |
| Perimeter | firewall, DDoS scrubbing, WAF |
| Network | segmentation, IDS or IPS, NAC |
| Endpoint | patched OS, antivirus or EDR, disk encryption |
| Application | secure coding, parameterised queries, updates |
| Data | encryption at rest and in transit, backups |
| People | training, phishing drills, least privilege |

[important]
Depth is not buying one of everything — it is assuming any single
layer will fail and making the next layer cheap to reach. A phished
password plus MFA, plus a stateful firewall, plus offline backups,
plus an alerting IPS is how real breaches stay small.
[/important]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | Remember this |
| --- | --- |
| Passive vs active attack | eavesdropping vs altering, flooding, injecting |
| Botnet | hijacked devices acting as one flood |
| SYN flood | half-open connections exhaust the table |
| DNS amplification | small query, huge reply, spoofed source |
| MitM on LAN | ARP poisoning routes traffic through attacker |
| Stateful firewall | tracks conversations, defaults to deny |
| IDS vs IPS | alerts on copies vs blocks inline |
| Zero trust | never trust, always verify, least privilege |
| VPN | encrypted tunnel against local snooping |
| Defence in depth | overlapping layers assume single failure |
